concrt140 and msvcp90: the concurrent_vector fixes from upstream Wine.

Backport of two upstream commits by Piotr Caban, unchanged:
de43f5434fe "concrt140: Sync details.c file with msvcp90." (2026-05-01) and
the msvcp90 half of d5c7ea9089e "msvcp120: Don't count segments being
allocated when reporting vector capacity." (2026-04-30). CrossOver 26.3's
dlls/concrt140/details.c is the upstream file from before de43f5434fe, byte
for byte, so the first applies as is.

concrt140's copy of the concurrent_vector code had missed msvcp90's 2025 fixes:
it sized segments as element_size times the count the allocator already
multiplies, and placed segments and elements with BYTE** arithmetic, eight
times too far. And in both copies _Internal_capacity counted a segment another
thread was still allocating (SEGMENT_ALLOC_MARKER, the value 1) as allocated,
so _Internal_reserve could return early and _Internal_push_back handed out the
marker plus an offset as the new element's address.

Forza Horizon 6's Steam build of 2026-11-03 (26261073) imports
?_Internal_push_back@_Concurrent_vector_base_v4@details@Concurrency@@IEAAPEAX_KAEA_K@Z
from concrt140 and stores through the result at forzahorizon6.exe+0xeb33ff.
On the Wine 11 engines r28 and r30 it crashed in its first minute with an
unhandled write to 0x9 or 0x29 (the marker plus 8, the marker plus two
16-byte elements plus 8): 5 of 10 cold starts on r28 and every one on r30, an
M4 with macOS 27.0, 2026-11-04. With Microsoft's concrt140 (concrt140=n,b) the
same environment ran 6 of 6, three on each, to the game's next screen. The
arm64 engines' Wine 11.18 already carries both commits.
